---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Invite from "../components/Invite.astro";
import InviteField from "../components/InviteField.astro";
import { BOOK_LINK } from "../consts";
import { homeGraph } from "../jsonld";
---

<BaseLayout structuredData={homeGraph}>
  <main id="content" class="mx-auto max-w-[640px] px-4 pt-8 pb-6 sm:px-6 sm:pt-12 sm:pb-8">
    <article>
      <h1 class="font-serif-display mb-3 text-3xl leading-[1.1] tracking-tight sm:text-[2.5rem]">
        No Agenda<span class="text-accent">,</span> No Meeting<span class="text-accent">.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mb-10 text-lg text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
        Please don't send meeting invites without an agenda.
      </p>

      <p
        class="mb-8 text-[1.125rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium text-gray-700 sm:text-xl dark:text-gray-300"
      >
        Imagine getting a calendar invite that just says
        <em class="not-italic underline decoration-[#e74c3c] underline-offset-[3px]"
          >"Quick sync"</em
        >
        with zero context&hellip; and having no idea what it's about. 🤦
      </p>

      <section aria-labelledby="dont-do-this">
        <h2
          id="dont-do-this"
          class="mt-10 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight"
        >
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[1.3rem]">❌</span> Don't do this
        </h2>

        <Invite
          variant="bad"
          subject="Quick sync"
          reaction="Am I in trouble? Do I need to prepare? What is this even about? 😰"
        >
          <InviteField label="When" value="Tuesday, 2:00 – 2:30 PM" />
          <InviteField label="Agenda" />
          <InviteField label="Context" />
          <InviteField label="Goals" />
        </Invite>

        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          You've just forced everyone on the invite to context-switch <em>twice</em>&mdash;once to
          figure out what this is about, and again in the meeting when they arrive unprepared. A
          meeting without an agenda is like calling a function without documentation&mdash;you don't
          know what inputs to prepare or what outputs to expect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="try-this-instead">
        <h2
          id="try-this-instead"
          class="mt-10 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight"
        >
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[1.3rem]">✅</span> Try this instead
        </h2>

        <Invite
          variant="good"
          subject="Q3 launch: go/no-go decision"
          reaction="Everyone arrives prepared. The meeting is focused. Better decisions get made. ✨"
        >
          <InviteField label="When" value="Tuesday, 2:00 – 2:30 PM" />
          <div class="mt-3 border-t border-gray-100 pt-3 dark:border-gray-700">
            <div
              class="mb-1 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wider text-gray-500 uppercase dark:text-gray-400"
            >
              Agenda
            </div>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <li class="py-0.5">Current status of API migration (5 min)</li>
              <li class="py-0.5">Open questions on the deployment window (10 min)</li>
              <li class="py-0.5">Decision: go/no-go for the beta release (15 min)</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-2 text-sm">
            <span aria-hidden="true">📎</span> Read-ahead:
            <span class="text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">Q3 launch status doc</span>
          </div>
        </Invite>

        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Notice how the second invite respects everyone's time? Attendees can prepare, the meeting
          stays on track, and you might even finish early.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr class="my-10 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700" />

      <section aria-labelledby="why-this-matters">
        <h2
          id="why-this-matters"
          class="mt-10 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight"
        >
          Why this matters
        </h2>

        <div
          class="my-6 rounded-r-lg border-l-4 border-blue-500 bg-blue-50 px-5 py-4 text-[0.95rem] dark:bg-blue-950"
        >
          The average knowledge worker spends
          <strong class="text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-300">40% of their workweek in meetings</strong
          >&mdash;that's two full days before they even start the "real work."
        </div>

        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">Every agenda-less meeting carries hidden costs:</p>

        <ul class="arrow-list">
          <li>
            People show up unprepared, so the first 10 minutes is spent catching everyone
            up&mdash;information that could have been shared as a document.
          </li>
          <li>
            Without clear goals, meetings drift. You end 30 minutes later with no decisions and a
            vague promise to "circle back."
          </li>
          <li>
            Context-switching to and from a mystery meeting destroys the deep focus work that
            creates actual value.
          </li>
          <li>
            If the organizer hasn't distilled their thoughts into writing, why should they get to
            decide how <em>you</em> spend your time?
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <hr class="my-10 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700" />

      <section aria-labelledby="what-to-do-instead">
        <h2
          id="what-to-do-instead"
          class="mt-10 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight"
        >
          What to do instead
        </h2>

        <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-4 text-lg leading-snug font-medium dark:text-gray-200">
          Before you schedule a meeting, ask: "Can this start as a document instead?"
        </p>

        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          You'd be surprised how often it can. A few minutes of writing often replaces waiting days
          for mutual availability and a 30-minute meeting. If you've done the writing and still need
          to meet, you've just created the agenda.
        </p>

        <ul class="arrow-list">
          <li>
            <strong>No agenda? Decline politely</strong>&mdash;or at least ask for one. It's well
            within your rights.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Write first, meet second.</strong> Transfer context asynchronously and schedule a
            meeting only if the conversation requires it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Include clear goals.</strong> What decision needs to be made? What question needs
            to be answered? If you can't articulate it, you don't need a meeting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Attach a read-ahead.</strong> Let people prepare on their own time instead of draining
            the first 15 minutes on information transfer.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Writing scales; meetings don't. A well-written document can reach hundreds of people at
          their convenience without interrupting anyone's flow. A meeting reaches only the people in
          the room, only at that exact moment.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <hr class="my-10 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700" />
  </main>

  <footer class="mx-auto max-w-[640px] px-4 pb-8 sm:px-6">
    <div
      class="my-6 rounded-lg border border-dashed border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 px-5 py-4 text-center text-sm text-gray-600 dark:border-gray-700 dark:bg-gray-800 dark:text-gray-400"
    >
      Got an invite without an agenda? Share this page:<br />
      <button
        type="button"
        data-copy-url="https://noagendanomeeting.net"
        class="mt-2 inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center rounded-md border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3 py-1 font-mono text-sm text-blue-700 transition-colors hover:border-blue-400 hover:bg-blue-50 dark:border-gray-700 dark:bg-gray-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:border-blue-500 dark:hover:bg-blue-950"
      >
        <span class="sr-only">Copy </span>noagendanomeeting.net
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="ml-1.5 text-gray-500">📋</span>
      </button>
      <span role="status" class="sr-only" data-copy-status></span>
    </div>

    <div class="book-promo mt-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-800 px-6 py-8 text-center text-white">
      <p class="book-promo-muted mb-2 text-sm text-slate-300 italic">Adapted from</p>
      <p class="font-serif-display mb-1 text-2xl leading-tight">
        <a href={BOOK_LINK} rel="noopener" class="text-white no-underline hover:underline"
          >Open <span class="text-accent">&amp;</span> Async</a
        >
      </p>
      <p class="book-promo-muted mt-2 text-sm text-slate-300">By Ben Balter</p>
      <p class="book-promo-muted mt-1 text-sm text-slate-300">
        A playbook for remote and distributed teams that actually work.
      </p>
      <a
        href={BOOK_LINK}
        rel="noopener"
        class="book-promo-cta mt-5 inline-block rounded-lg bg-blue-600 px-6 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white no-underline transition-colors hover:bg-blue-700"
        >Get the book &rarr;</a
      >
    </div>

    <p class="mt-4 text-center text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
      Inspired by
      <a
        href="https://nohello.net"
        rel="noopener"
        class="underline hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-300">nohello.net</a
      >
    </p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import { initClipboard } from "../clipboard";

    // Module scripts are deferred, so the DOM is already parsed here.
    initClipboard();
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
